  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Hooded Grouse Locust (Paratettix cucullatus) is a tiny member of the Pygmy Grasshoppers family, characterized by its diminutive size and cryptic coloration. These insects are among the smallest grasshoppers found in New England, typically measuring less than 12mm in length.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Hood-like Pronotum",
      "description": "The pronotum extends forward over the head like a hood or shield, which is the defining characteristic giving this species its common name",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Tiny Size",
      "description": "Exceptionally small grasshopper, usually under 12mm long, making it one of the smallest grasshoppers in the region",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Brown Coloration",
      "description": "Generally brown or tan coloration that blends well with leaf litter and soil, providing excellent camouflage",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Hooded Grouse Locusts prefer moist, leafy areas including forest floors, meadow margins, and areas with dense leaf litter where they can remain hidden and feed on decomposing vegetation.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite their grasshopper classification, Hooded Grouse Locusts have reduced wings and are mostly flightless, relying instead on their cryptic coloration and leaf-litter habitat to avoid predators.",
